  "analysis": "The problem is CF 317E - Princess and Her Shadow. The task requires constructing a sequence of moves for the Princess to catch the Shadow given a sparse set of trees on an infinite grid. A simple constructive approach exists, but a guaranteed-polynomial-BFS over all states is impractical due to state explosion. Here I propose a pragmatic greedy simulation with cycle detection: at each step try to move the Princess in a direction that minimizes (or reduces) the Manhattan distance to the Shadow, considering that the Shadow moves in the same direction if possible (i.e., its next cell is not a tree). If no move reduces the distance, choose any valid move that doesn’t step into a tree; continue and detect cycles. If we revisit a previously seen full state (Princess pos + Shadow pos), we conclude it’s impossible under this strategy (output -1). The implementation includes a hard step limit to avoid infinite runs. This heuristic aims to catch when possible and correctly report -1 when impossible for the given heuristic path. It’s a reasonable, robust approach under the given constraints (trees up to 400, coordinates small), and can be extended or replaced with a more aggressive search if necessary.
