  "analysis": "We need to reorder 2n cards so that the sequence of front numbers (a_i) and the sequence of back numbers (b_i) are both balanced bracket sequences. A known constructive approach uses the fact that for each front value t, there are exactly two cards containing t and -t on the front, and similarly for the back values. Build two perfect matchings on the set of cards: one matching connects the two cards that hold the same absolute value on the front (i.e., a_i = t and a_j = -t), and the other matching connects the two cards that hold the same absolute value on the back (i.e., b_i = t and b_j = -t). The union of these two matchings decomposes into disjoint cycles (each vertex/card has degree 2). If we traverse a cycle in one of the two possible orientations, the subsequence of cards from that cycle can be arranged to keep both fronts and backs balanced within that cycle. Since different cycles are independent with respect to bracket types, we can concatenate appropriately oriented cycles to obtain a global solution if one exists.

Plan:
- Build mappings to find for each card its front-partner (the card with -a_i) and its back-partner (the card with -b_i).
- Decompose the graph into cycles by walking alternately via front-edges and back-edges: F, B, F, B, ...
- For each cycle, generate two candidate orders: the cycle as found (order1) and its reversal (order2).
- Check if order1 balances both fronts and backs within that cycle; similarly check order2.
- If neither orientation balances both fronts and backs for that cycle, there is no global solution → print NO.
- Otherwise, pick the orientation that balances both for that cycle and append its cards to the final sequence.
- After processing all cycles, verify the entire front and back sequences are balanced (as a sanity check). If valid, print YES and the chosen ordering; otherwise print NO.

This approach runs in O(n) time and O(n) memory, and is robust up to n = 2e5.

