  "analysis": "We need to solve CF problem 382D - Ksenia and Pawns. The board is n x m with characters in {<, >, ^, v, #}. All border cells are '#'. Each non-# cell points to its neighbor in the direction of its arrow. '#' cells are sinks: once a pawn reaches a '#', it stays there. Two pawns cannot occupy the same non-# cell at the same time, but they may occupy the same '#' cell. If there is any directed cycle among non-# cells, the answer is -1 (infinite score) because two pawns can be placed on two different nodes of the cycle and move indefinitely without colliding on non-# cells. Otherwise, the graph is a DAG where every non-# cell has exactly one outgoing edge (to its neighbor, which may be '#'), and the '#' cells are sinks.

Key observations:
- If a cycle exists among non-# cells, answer is -1.
- If no cycle exists, every non-# cell eventually reaches some '#'. Define depth[v] as the number of moves before v reaches a '#'. Define sink[v] as the '#'-cell reached by v.
- For two pawns starting at nodes u and v:
  - If they belong to different sinks, they will never collide (different final sinks). The maximum possible total moves in this case is the sum of the maximum depths per sink, taking the two largest such values across distinct sinks.
  - If they belong to the same sink, they may collide at their first meeting node w (the LCA in the sink’s reverse tree) if and only if their depths are equal. Therefore, among nodes mapping to the same sink, you can take two starting depths as long as they are different. The maximum for a single sink is the sum of the two largest depths among nodes with that sink, but ensuring the two depths are different (the sink node has depth 0 and helps in forming a valid pair).

Algorithm:
1) Build the next array for every non-# cell, pointing to its neighbor in the direction of its arrow.
2) Detect cycles among non-# cells by following arrows with a color/visit state. If a cycle is found, print -1.
3) If no cycle, compute depth[v] and sink[v] for all cells via iterative path compression (no recursion due to large N). Initialize depth for '#' cells as 0 and sink as themselves.
4) For each sink, compute dmax[sink] = the maximum depth among all nodes that reach that sink.
5) Count how many sinks are actually used (i.e., have at least one node mapping to them). If there are at least two used sinks, answer is the sum of the two largest dmax across used sinks.
6) If there is only one used sink, compute the maximum sum for two starting nodes within that sink’s tree with distinct depths: take the two largest depths among all nodes with that sink but with different depth values; if only one depth exists, answer is 0.

This approach runs in O(n*m) time and uses O(n*m) memory, which fits the constraints (n,m up to 2000 => N up to 4e6).

