When we insert edges, the graph becomes tree + some extra edges. A key observation (true for this problem’s intended solution) is that the only w that can change their f_w when adding an edge (a, b) are the vertices on the path between a and b in the original tree; more precisely, adding (a, b) merges two components in G − w for each w on that path, changing the s_i for that w. If we keep track of, for every w, the current partition of V \ {w} into components with their sizes s_i and a flag k_i indicating how many edges from w enter that component (k_i ≥ 2 means the component contributes an extra term s_i*(s_i−1) in f_w), we can update f_w for each such w when merging two components along the path. The delta when merging two components of sizes a and b around w depends on whether those components already had k_i≥2, and is derived from the change in the two terms that make up f_w: the sum of squares of component sizes, and the sum over components with k_i≥2 of s_i*(s_i−1). For the basic case where both components had k_i = k_j = 1 prior to the merge (the common case when processing the first extra edge on that region), the delta for w simplifies to delta_f_w = a^2 + b^2 − (a + b). In full generality, one should use delta_f_w = −2ab + Delta_k where Delta_k accounts for the change in the k≥2 term (Delta_k = (a+b)(a+b−1) − [k_i≥2 ? a(a−1) : 0] − [k_j≥2 ? b(b−1) : 0] and k_new = k_i + k_j). This yields correct updates for all cases, including prior merges.

